Ari Huttunen writes:
>I would be more than satisfied if the whole concept of lawful/neutral/chaotic
>-something would be thrown out of the game. I didn't see any reason to add
>it to the game and I don't see any reason to keep it there. 

Are you joking?  Altars, artifacts, hell, priest(esse)s...  demons,
peaceful monsters... all of these things are affected by alignment.
What about blessings/curses; don't these add richness to the game?  I
find the whole alignment/diety relations part of the game one of the
best parts, surely more satisfying than hacking up another 50 orcs.

>In a game of
>D&D (tm) it is quite useful because that game is played by people against 
>people. Nethack is ruled by a computer that really can't understand what
>is lawful and what is chaotic.
>
>(Exactly how does nethack understand chaotic/lawful?)

There are some differences in alignments, such as the specific
artifact sets available, and which monsters will sometimes be peaceful
towards you.  The only *real* difference between alignments is that
chaotics can pray in hell, and lawfuls can convert non-co-aligned
altars to holy-water factories.  That is it, as far as I know.

I (for one) would like to see much more distinction between the
benefits that players get for being various alignments.  As it is, any
alignment will give artifacts; any player can get holy water at a
Lawful altar; any character can get Excaliber; any character can use
any artifact; all priest(esse)s bestow the same special powers; all
the gods will act the same way when you pray, etc.  

Thus, currently alignment is basically a trinary classification scheme
used to generate arbitrary mismatches between, say, a player and a
particular altar she has found.  This, I believe, is what Mr Huttunen
is decrying, and I must agree with him.  Alignments should have
meaning outside of their relationships to each other -- being lawful
should mean more than simply being non-neutral and non-chaotic.
*That* is the general idea I had in mind when contributing to the
latest discussion of artifacts; since they have alignment, that
alignment should *matter*.  Wouldn't it make a hack much more
interesting game if there were real importance attached to your
alignment?

Here are a bunch of ways I can think of to make alignment be a little
more meaningful and interesting.  Feel free to add your own.

1) Penalize lawfuls for even *attempting* to rob a shop or vault, or
hitting a peaceful monster.  Reward chaotics for the same.  No benefit
either way for neutrals.

2) Allow only lawful gods to bless characters' items when they pray.

3) Expand the range of altar gifts available.  Chaotic gods would
preferentially give artifacts, attack wands, and other fighting type
material.  Lawful gods usually give blessed extra-healing potions,
blessed food, armor, and other more defensive materials.

4) Have lawful altars only bless water for lawful (or maybe neutral)
characters.  

5) Have chaotic gods be quite unreliable about listening to prayers;
the timeout should be much less (say, 50 moves) but the chance of
having the god listen be much less also.  Most of the time you would
see: "You get the feeling that Dis is not listening" or some such.
Give neutrals half the normal timeout and half the chance of being
heard; lawful would stay the same.

6) Penalize the use of non-coaligned artifacts.

7) Have lawful sentient monsters (dwarves, elves) occasionally become
tamed to lawful characters, and thereby work with the player to kill
the baddies on the level.  Have this work very infrequently for
neutrals, and never for chaotics.  The tamed creature should not
necessarily stay tame forever; maybe becoming peaceful as soon as an
orc infestation is eliminated.

8) Have lawful monsters occasionally attack chaotics; have chaotic
monsters occasionally attack anything.  

9) In line with (8) and (1), have monsters sometimes feign
peacefulness -- a guaranteed headache for lawfuls who are bound by
their god not to attack first, even if they know the monsters may
attack freely at any moment!

10) Make priest(esse)s *prevent* any non-coaligned person from using
their altar.  This is simple to do: make them stand on the altar the
way shopkeepers stand on their doorway whenever a character is within
two squares.  Is a lawful priest going to stand there while Joe Piddly
make a human sacrifice?  Hardly likely.

11) Change the way gods respond to prayer.  Chaotics will gate in
demons to help you, add plusses to your weapons and armor, maybe smite
your enemies by hand, curse your enemies somehow, and this sort of
thing.  Then they might worry about your hunger.  Lawful gods will be
much more likely to fully heal you, feed you, bless you, maybe boost
your luck, temporarily lower your armor class 10 points, and then worry
about trying to help you fight.

12) Make the prayer of lawfuls work as a weapon or protective
mechanism versus demons and the undead.

13) Make lawful gods (and to some extent, neutral ones) liable to help
you when you pray even if the timeout is not up.  They may very well
help you and smite you at the same time, i.e:
#pray
You get the feeling that Athena is angry! -more-
The lightning hits the master lich! -more-
The master lich dies! -more-
Athena heals you! -more-
"Mortal, it saddens me that you have not learned your lessons." -more-
Goodbye, level 12.

